; `addChecked`, `subChecked` and `mulChecked` at every boundary they
; have, because for an arithmetic operator the boundaries ARE the
; feature. Everything in the middle works by construction; what a
; checked operator is for is the four values where `+`, `-` and `*`
; stop telling the truth.
;
; WHY THESE EXIST AT ALL. The three emit plain `add`/`sub`/`mul` with no
; `nsw`, so overflow wraps silently. `stdlib/Rpc.ax` carries the
; measurement of what that costs on a security boundary: a peer's
; `Content-Length` added to the framer's own offset is "the one quantity
; here an attacker can push past i64; formed once, it wraps NEGATIVE",
; the wait-for-body test then passes on its first evaluation, and the
; reader slices a body that never arrived - 65,497 bytes of
; uninitialised buffer, handed to the peer. That site was repaired by
; hand, by never forming the sum. The repair does not generalise; these
; do, and the last term below is that exact shape asked as a question.
;
; `intMin` IS THE VALUE THAT BREAKS EVERY RULE, and it gets its own
; terms in all three. It has no positive counterpart, so negating it
; overflows, subtracting it from zero overflows, and multiplying it by
; -1 overflows - and that last one is the case `mulChecked`'s division
; test structurally CANNOT see, because the wrapped product is `intMin`
; again and dividing it by -1 is itself undefined (MM-VAL-3b). It is
; excluded before the division rather than caught after it, and both
; operand orders are excluded, because `(* -1 intMin)` is the same
; overflow written the other way round.
;
; `remChecked` and `shrChecked` below close the fixture gap
; `docs/error-model.md` ERR-REC-2 named: every other checked operator
; had boundary terms here and these two had none. The remainder's one
; wraparound is `intMin % -1` - the only `(a, b)` whose mathematical
; value does not fit, and on some platforms a trap rather than a
; value, so it is excluded the way `mulChecked` excludes its own
; unseeable case. The shift's boundary is the amount, not the value:
; 63 is the widest exact shift and 64 the first refusal, with the
; negative amount refused beside it.
;
; AND THE WHOLE FILE ANSWERS THE SAME AT EVERY `--opt` LEVEL, which is
; the property the raw operators cannot claim: `MM-VAL-3b` records
; `INT_MIN / -1` and shifts of 64 or more answering differently at 0
; and 1. Measured here at 0, 1, 2 and 3 - byte-identical stdout at all
; four. That is what a checked operator is FOR, and it is the reason
; `mulChecked` excludes the undefined division rather than relying on
; what it happens to return.
(import IO)

(import Err)

(:: say (-> Int String Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (say okFlag label)
  (if (== okFlag 1)
    (printlnLit (strCStr (strConcat "ok   " label)))
    (printlnLit (strCStr (strConcat "FAIL " label)))
  )
)

; The binder is handed to a function whose parameter is DECLARED
; `Error` because, when this was written, a `match` binder over a
; polymorphic scrutinee did not carry its instantiated type and
; reading `e.code` in the arm was AX3004.
;
; THE SENTENCE THAT USED TO END THIS PARAGRAPH WAS WRONG, and wrong in
; the way this repository names most often. It said "this file would
; stop compiling if it stopped being needed, which is the good
; direction to fail in" - a claim that the workaround is load-bearing
; and that its removal would announce itself. It is not and it did
; not. `ctorPatEnv` lifted the limitation on 2026-08-21 and this file
; went on compiling, because a program written to avoid a refusal
; cannot notice the refusal being lifted. A fixture cannot pin the
; ABSENCE of a diagnostic by not provoking it.
;
; What actually pins it is `371-err-module.ax` term 2, which runs both
; spellings and compares their answers, and `docs/error-model.md`
; ERR-TYPE-3a records the retirement. `errCode` stays here: it is a
; public name and this file is about checked arithmetic, not about
; which way an error's fields are read.
(:: codeOf (-> (Result Int Error) Int))

(fn (codeOf r)
  (match r
    ((Ok x) 0)
    ((Err e) (errCode e))
  )
)

(:: valueOr (-> (Result Int Error) Int Int))

(fn (valueOr r d)
  (match r
    ((Ok x) x)
    ((Err e) d)
  )
)

; Overflowed, specifically - not merely failed. A checked operator that
; answered `errDivideByZero` for a sum would satisfy `isErr` and be
; wrong, so every refusal below is asked for its code.
(:: overflowed (-> (Result Int Error) Int))

(fn (overflowed r)
  (if (== (codeOf r) errOverflow)
    1
    0
  )
)

(:: gave (-> (Result Int Error) Int Int))

(fn (gave r want)
  (if (isOk r)
    (if (== (valueOr r 0) want)
      1
      0
    )
    0
  )
)

; Refused with a SPECIFIC code - not merely failed. `divChecked` has
; two refusals with two meanings (zero divisor against unrepresentable
; quotient) and the shift has its own, so `isErr` alone would accept a
; swapped diagnostic the way `overflowed` would accept a swapped sum.
(:: refused (-> (Result Int Error) Int Int))

(fn (refused r want)
  (if (isErr r)
    (if (== (codeOf r) want)
      1
      0
    )
    0
  )
)

(:: intMax Int)

(fn (intMax) 9223372036854775807)

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  ; --- addition ---------------------------------------------------
  {
    (say (overflowed (addChecked intMax 1)) "intMax + 1 overflows")
    (say (overflowed (addChecked intMin (- 0 1))) "intMin + -1 overflows")
    (say (overflowed (addChecked intMax intMax)) "intMax + intMax overflows")
    (say (gave (addChecked intMax (- 0 1)) (- intMax 1)) "intMax + -1 is exact")
    (say (gave (addChecked intMin 1) (+ intMin 1)) "intMin + 1 is exact")
    (say (gave (addChecked 0 0) 0) "0 + 0 is exact")
    ; Operands of opposite sign can never overflow, however large, and
    ; a check that flagged them would be as wrong as one that missed a
    ; real overflow.
    (say (gave (addChecked intMax intMin) (- 0 1)) "intMax + intMin is exact, not a refusal")
    ; --- subtraction ------------------------------------------------
    (say (overflowed (subChecked intMin 1)) "intMin - 1 overflows")
    (say (overflowed (subChecked intMax (- 0 1))) "intMax - -1 overflows")
    (say (overflowed (subChecked 0 intMin)) "0 - intMin overflows, because intMin has no negation")
    (say (gave (subChecked intMin (- 0 1)) (+ intMin 1)) "intMin - -1 is exact")
    (say (gave (subChecked 5 3) 2) "5 - 3 is exact")
    (say (gave (subChecked intMin intMin) 0) "intMin - intMin is exact, not a refusal")
    ; --- multiplication ---------------------------------------------
    ; The two orders of the case the division test cannot see.
    (say (overflowed (mulChecked intMin (- 0 1))) "intMin * -1 overflows")
    (say (overflowed (mulChecked (- 0 1) intMin)) "-1 * intMin overflows, in either order")
    (say (overflowed (mulChecked intMax 2)) "intMax * 2 overflows")
    (say (overflowed (mulChecked intMin 2)) "intMin * 2 overflows")
    (say (overflowed (mulChecked 4294967296 4294967296)) "2^32 * 2^32 overflows")
    ; Zero is answered before the division, which would otherwise be by
    ; zero - and it must be answered for BOTH operands.
    (say (gave (mulChecked 0 intMin) 0) "0 * intMin is exact")
    (say (gave (mulChecked intMin 0) 0) "intMin * 0 is exact")
    (say (gave (mulChecked intMin 1) intMin) "intMin * 1 is exact")
    (say (gave (mulChecked intMax 1) intMax) "intMax * 1 is exact")
    (say (gave (mulChecked (- 0 1) (- 0 1)) 1) "-1 * -1 is exact")
    (say (gave (mulChecked 3 5) 15) "3 * 5 is exact")
    ; --- remainder --------------------------------------------------
    ; The one wraparound, excluded up front like mulChecked's: intMin
    ; % -1 is the only pair whose value does not fit.
    (say (refused (remChecked intMin (- 0 1)) errOverflow) "intMin % -1 is unrepresentable")
    (say (refused (remChecked 10 0) errDivideByZero) "x % 0 is a zero divisor, not an overflow")
    (say (gave (remChecked 10 3) 1) "10 % 3 is exact")
    (say (gave (remChecked intMin 1) 0) "intMin % 1 is exact, not a refusal")
    (say (gave (remChecked 0 5) 0) "0 % 5 is exact")
    ; --- shift ------------------------------------------------------
    ; The boundary is the amount: 63 shifts, 64 refuses, and so does a
    ; negative one. intMin >> 63 is exact - arithmetic shift of the
    ; sign bit down to the units place - which is the term that would
    ; go red if the check were `> 63` instead of `>= 64`.
    (say (refused (shrChecked 1 64) errShiftTooWide) "1 >> 64 refuses")
    (say (refused (shrChecked 1 100) errShiftTooWide) "1 >> 100 refuses")
    (say (refused (shrChecked 1 (- 0 1)) errShiftTooWide) "1 >> -1 refuses")
    (say (gave (shrChecked intMin 63) (- 0 1)) "intMin >> 63 is exact")
    (say (gave (shrChecked 8 2) 2) "8 >> 2 is exact")
    (say (gave (shrChecked 5 0) 5) "5 >> 0 is exact")
    ; --- the shape the repository already measured ------------------
    ; `bodyRel + n`, where `n` is the peer's number. Unchecked this
    ; wraps negative and the framer slices a body that never arrived.
    ; Asked as a question it is a refusal, and the refusal is a value
    ; the caller can act on rather than a wrong length it cannot see.
    (say (overflowed (addChecked 64 (- intMax 8))) "a framer offset plus a peer's length refuses instead of wrapping")
    0
  }
)
